Pedro Pathing Visualizer
------------------------
https://github.com/Pedro-Pathing/Visualizer
Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.
Full licence text: web/public/third-party/pedro-visualizer-LICENSE.txt

Its own NOTICE records that it contains significant portions of a fork by
Matthew Allen, also under Apache-2.0.

What was taken, and what was changed:

  * The motion model in web/src/lib/pedro.ts. The Visualizer models a drive as
    having separate forward and strafe top speeds and separate acceleration and
    braking limits; this planner previously assumed a single top speed, which
    over-estimated how fast a sideways leg could be driven. Its default figures
    (75 in/s forward, 65 in/s strafe, 30 in/s^2 either way) are used as our
    defaults.

  * Its observation that the usable field span is 141.5 inches rather than the
    full 144 of six 24-inch tiles.

  * The idea of tracing the robot's body along the route (it calls these onion
    layers). Ours colours a trace when that pose would hit an obstacle or leave
    the field, which its version does not do.

  * Multiple named, coloured path chains, its PATH CHAINS control with New /
    Duplicate / Remove, and the idea that each chain is followed in sequence.
    Our emitted Java differs: one PathChain per chain inside a LinearOpMode
    that follows each and waits on isBusy between them.

  * Its layout: a toolbar carrying the elapsed/total clock and the distance,
    with icon actions beside it; a control column of sections that state their
    own counts; a live robot-position readout above an editable starting point.
    Rebuilt in this project's own components and palette — none of its Svelte,
    CSS or colour scheme is used.

  Rewritten rather than copied: the implementation here is TypeScript against
  this project's own path model, not a port of the Visualizer's Svelte source.
  The elliptical direction-to-speed function and the asymmetric trapezoidal
  profile are our own formulation of the same idea.

  Not taken: the Visualizer's field images (decode.webp, intothedeep.webp,
  centerstage.webp). Apache-2.0 covers that repository's own work; the FTC
  field artwork depicted is FIRST's, and the licence does not grant rights to
  it.
